WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:07.920
- Good afternoon everyone.

00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:10.020
I am Kristina Talbert-Slagle,

00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:12.690
Assistant Professor at the
Yale School of Medicine

00:00:12.690 --> 00:00:15.210
and Director of the
Faculty Support Initiative

00:00:15.210 --> 00:00:18.240
at the Yale Institute for Global Health.

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:20.730
I am also the Principal Investigator

00:00:20.730 --> 00:00:23.730
for Applying Research
for a Healthy Liberia,

00:00:23.730 --> 00:00:25.980
a five-year collaborative project

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:29.354
with the University of Liberia
College of Health Sciences

00:00:29.354 --> 00:00:31.650
and Vanderbilt University,

00:00:31.650 --> 00:00:35.250
funded by the U.S. Agency for
International Development.

00:00:35.250 --> 00:00:37.320
I am delighted to welcome you

00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:40.260
to this Global Health
Conversation series event,

00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:45.260
Ebola Ten Years On, Reflections
and Lessons Learned.

00:00:45.750 --> 00:00:47.730
in addition to the Yale Institute

00:00:47.730 --> 00:00:49.800
for Global Health, or YIGH,

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:53.790
today's event is co-sponsored
by the Walker Fund

00:00:53.790 --> 00:00:56.220
and the Council on African Studies

00:00:56.220 --> 00:00:58.743
at the MacMillan Center at Yale.

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:03.210
This December marks
the 10 year anniversary

00:01:03.210 --> 00:01:04.950
of the emergence of Ebola

00:01:04.950 --> 00:01:07.410
in the West African country of Guinea,

00:01:07.410 --> 00:01:10.410
an event that caused a tragic epidemic

00:01:10.410 --> 00:01:13.440
and more than 10,000 deaths in Guinea

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:17.310
and its neighboring countries,
Liberia and Sierra Leone.

00:01:17.310 --> 00:01:20.670
Since the 2013 to '15 Ebola epidemic,

00:01:20.670 --> 00:01:24.300
the world has experienced
numerous global health threats,

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:28.890
such as further Ebola
outbreaks, Marburg, measles,

00:01:28.890 --> 00:01:31.233
and COVID-19, among others.

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:35.940
During this webinar, three
senior health leaders

00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:39.090
who responded to the
Ebola epidemic in Liberia,

00:01:39.090 --> 00:01:42.420
Guinea, and Sierra Leone
will share their reflections

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:45.330
on the weaknesses in
healthcare, public health,

00:01:45.330 --> 00:01:46.890
and health financing

00:01:46.890 --> 00:01:50.370
that exacerbated the Ebola
crisis in their countries,

00:01:50.370 --> 00:01:53.130
as well as lessons learned that can be,

00:01:53.130 --> 00:01:54.300
or have already been,

00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:56.760
applied to respond better

00:01:56.760 --> 00:01:59.430
to emerging infectious disease threats,

00:01:59.430 --> 00:02:01.920
build more resilient health systems,

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:05.190
and improve health outcomes globally.

00:02:05.190 --> 00:02:08.850
It is our pleasure to have
three distinguished guests

00:02:08.850 --> 00:02:10.230
joining us today,

00:02:10.230 --> 00:02:14.430
all of whom are esteemed
leaders in global public health.

00:02:14.430 --> 00:02:16.770
To formally introduce our panelists,

00:02:16.770 --> 00:02:20.850
I will now turn to my
colleague, Dr. Sabine Franklin.

00:02:20.850 --> 00:02:21.870
- Hello everyone.

00:02:21.870 --> 00:02:24.420
My name is Dr. Sabine Franklin,

00:02:24.420 --> 00:02:26.760
and I'm a Mellon Postdoctoral Fellow

00:02:26.760 --> 00:02:30.690
in the Africana Studies
Department at Wellesley College.

00:02:30.690 --> 00:02:34.440
It is my pleasure to introduce
our distinguished panel

00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:36.213
and to moderate today's event.

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:41.310
Dr. Bernice Dahn was the
Minister of Health of Liberia

00:02:41.310 --> 00:02:44.190
from 2015 to 2018,

00:02:44.190 --> 00:02:47.880
and previously served as
the Chief Medical Officer

00:02:47.880 --> 00:02:52.290
of Liberia from 2006 to 2015,

00:02:52.290 --> 00:02:55.260
where she oversaw post-war health systems

00:02:55.260 --> 00:02:57.690
strengthening efforts.

00:02:57.690 --> 00:02:59.790
She is currently the Vice President

00:02:59.790 --> 00:03:03.090
for Health Sciences at
the University of Liberia,

00:03:03.090 --> 00:03:04.770
leading the College of Sciences,

00:03:04.770 --> 00:03:07.260
which houses the only schools of medicine

00:03:07.260 --> 00:03:09.510
and pharmacy in the country,

00:03:09.510 --> 00:03:11.850
as well as the New School of Public Health

00:03:11.850 --> 00:03:13.953
and Nursing Midwifery Program.

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:18.420
Dr. Dahn has authored
more than 40 publications,

00:03:18.420 --> 00:03:20.310
spearheaded an investment plan

00:03:20.310 --> 00:03:22.410
for building a resilient health system

00:03:22.410 --> 00:03:24.930
in Liberia post Ebola,

00:03:24.930 --> 00:03:27.600
and is internationally recognized expert

00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:30.873
in health system strengthening
and pandemic recovery.

00:03:33.270 --> 00:03:36.690
Dr. Sylla Thiam is a
public health specialist

00:03:36.690 --> 00:03:39.060
with more than 20 years experience

00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:42.690
in health management and
leadership across Africa.

00:03:42.690 --> 00:03:45.120
He is currently an independent consultant

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:47.490
and a part-time research associate

00:03:47.490 --> 00:03:50.820
at the University of Thies in Senegal.

00:03:50.820 --> 00:03:53.370
He has been with Amref Health Africa,

00:03:53.370 --> 00:03:56.820
the largest healthcare
nonprofit on the continent

00:03:56.820 --> 00:04:00.090
from 2011 until 2018,

00:04:00.090 --> 00:04:05.090
when he was the regional
director of Amref in West Africa.

00:04:05.340 --> 00:04:07.950
Prior to that, he worked
with the Ministry of Health

00:04:07.950 --> 00:04:10.380
in Senegal in various positions

00:04:10.380 --> 00:04:14.400
at district and national levels
leading various campaigns,

00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:17.700
notably the reduction of the malaria cases

00:04:17.700 --> 00:04:20.850
via systemic changes to
the ministry's surveillance

00:04:20.850 --> 00:04:22.833
and delivery of services.

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:28.350
Paramount Chief, David Mandu
Farley Kelli-Coomber IV

00:04:28.350 --> 00:04:30.480
is an experienced engineer

00:04:30.480 --> 00:04:33.150
who spent two decades in the United States

00:04:33.150 --> 00:04:36.630
before returning to Sierra Leone in 2008

00:04:36.630 --> 00:04:39.060
to work in the mining sector.

00:04:39.060 --> 00:04:42.030
He was elected to head the Mandu Chiefdom

00:04:42.030 --> 00:04:47.030
in the Kailahun district
on December 2nd, 2013.

00:04:47.130 --> 00:04:50.820
A few weeks later, the first
case of the Ebola virus disease

00:04:50.820 --> 00:04:54.990
in a child occurred in
Gueckedou prefecture in Guinea,

00:04:54.990 --> 00:04:57.540
which borders the Kailahun District,

00:04:57.540 --> 00:05:00.180
and it ultimately caused the child's death

00:05:00.180 --> 00:05:05.180
and triggered the 2013 to 2016 pandemic.

00:05:05.310 --> 00:05:08.250
Thus, one of his first
governance challenge

00:05:08.250 --> 00:05:11.220
was to implement emergency bylaws.

00:05:11.220 --> 00:05:13.470
He convinced other paramount chiefs

00:05:13.470 --> 00:05:16.680
in the Kailahun district to
implement the same bylaws,

00:05:16.680 --> 00:05:19.530
to discourage movement, close contact,

00:05:19.530 --> 00:05:22.230
and encourage help seeking behaviors.

00:05:22.230 --> 00:05:23.580
These were later scaled up

00:05:23.580 --> 00:05:26.250
and implemented as part of Sierra Leone's

00:05:26.250 --> 00:05:30.510
National Emergency
Declaration in August 2014.

00:05:30.510 --> 00:05:32.760
So, welcome to all of our panelists.

00:05:32.760 --> 00:05:34.910
It is great to have
you here with us today.

00:05:37.230 --> 00:05:39.990
So we are moving now to the first round,

00:05:39.990 --> 00:05:42.720
and I ask each panelist this question.

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:47.310
It has been 10 years and tens
of thousands of articles,

00:05:47.310 --> 00:05:50.820
photos, and reports on
the deadliest outbreak

00:05:50.820 --> 00:05:53.760
of the Ebola virus disease.

00:05:53.760 --> 00:05:58.200
What parts of the narrative
or the story is missing?

00:05:58.200 --> 00:06:01.590
And can you tell us that
story in two minutes?

00:06:01.590 --> 00:06:03.303
I will start with Dr. Dahn.

00:06:07.500 --> 00:06:11.190
- Thank you and hello to everyone

00:06:11.190 --> 00:06:13.533
who is on to listen.

00:06:18.294 --> 00:06:20.387
So, it's 10 years after the outbreak,

00:06:24.300 --> 00:06:27.280
and during the outbreak

00:06:28.350 --> 00:06:29.580
there was this momentum

00:06:29.580 --> 00:06:32.523
to build resilient
healthcare delivery systems,

00:06:33.420 --> 00:06:38.130
to ensure that the impact of outbreaks

00:06:38.130 --> 00:06:40.590
will not be felt like the way it was felt

00:06:40.590 --> 00:06:42.813
during the Ebola outbreak for countries.

00:06:43.710 --> 00:06:46.770
And countries should be better prepared

00:06:46.770 --> 00:06:50.193
for new emergencies.

00:06:53.040 --> 00:06:57.333
Which part of the story
that is not being told,

00:06:59.850 --> 00:07:04.850
there was momentum to support countries,

00:07:06.480 --> 00:07:07.923
strengthening countries,

00:07:09.780 --> 00:07:13.443
along building the resilient
healthcare delivery systems.

00:07:14.940 --> 00:07:19.940
Countries supported
countries that were weak

00:07:22.620 --> 00:07:24.096
to help them build

00:07:24.096 --> 00:07:25.920
the resilient healthcare delivery systems.

00:07:25.920 --> 00:07:27.423
Promises were made.

00:07:28.500 --> 00:07:31.203
Some of those promises were not fulfilled.

00:07:32.490 --> 00:07:36.453
Not every promise that was
made was a new promise,

00:07:38.190 --> 00:07:39.933
and moving onwards,

00:07:41.580 --> 00:07:46.430
whether countries still
align to the efforts

00:07:47.370 --> 00:07:50.820
of building the resilience
that we all felt

00:07:50.820 --> 00:07:52.150
should have been built

00:07:53.190 --> 00:07:57.513
within the outbreak of
Ebola and moving on.

00:07:59.430 --> 00:08:02.170
So for me, I will summarize that to that

00:08:03.030 --> 00:08:05.130
and wait for the next question, thank you.

00:08:07.530 --> 00:08:09.450
- Thank you, Dr. Dahn.

00:08:09.450 --> 00:08:11.853
So we'll move on to Dr. Thiam.

00:08:12.750 --> 00:08:15.660
Could you please tell us
what parts of the story

00:08:15.660 --> 00:08:18.610
or the narrative is
missing in two minutes?

00:08:21.369 --> 00:08:22.367
- Okay.

00:08:22.367 --> 00:08:26.617
(Sylla Thiam, speaking in French)

00:09:55.980 --> 00:09:58.920
- Paramount Chief
Farley, could you tell us

00:09:58.920 --> 00:10:02.400
what part of the narratives
or the story is missing?

00:10:02.400 --> 00:10:05.163
And could you please tell
us that in two minutes?

00:10:06.030 --> 00:10:07.060
- Two issues.

00:10:07.060 --> 00:10:10.920
There are many, but two principle issues.

00:10:10.920 --> 00:10:15.450
One is how important the
governance structure,

00:10:15.450 --> 00:10:20.450
governance issues are to properly
address emergent diseases,

00:10:23.760 --> 00:10:27.600
and specifically a problem like Ebola.

00:10:27.600 --> 00:10:30.003
I can give examples of that later on.

00:10:31.680 --> 00:10:33.240
That's the first issue.

00:10:33.240 --> 00:10:34.773
The second issue is that,

00:10:36.990 --> 00:10:39.750
in terms of local populations,

00:10:39.750 --> 00:10:43.803
Ebola was a very severe crisis,

00:10:45.630 --> 00:10:48.963
but keeping it in perspective,

00:10:50.130 --> 00:10:55.130
there are still normal
diseases that are endemic here

00:10:56.220 --> 00:10:58.900
that probably that kill
so many more people

00:10:59.940 --> 00:11:04.940
long after the international
efforts have been

00:11:05.130 --> 00:11:06.750
very well appreciated.

00:11:06.750 --> 00:11:09.840
International efforts
were made to combat Ebola.

00:11:09.840 --> 00:11:14.310
I'm talking specifically
about malaria and typhoid,

00:11:14.310 --> 00:11:16.860
which continues, unfortunately,

00:11:16.860 --> 00:11:19.500
to take a very high mortality toll

00:11:19.500 --> 00:11:21.063
on our regular populations.

00:11:22.200 --> 00:11:25.920
And so while we are very
happy about the attention

00:11:25.920 --> 00:11:30.920
that was brought to our
healthcare issues by Ebola,

00:11:32.700 --> 00:11:34.680
by the Ebola epidemic,

00:11:34.680 --> 00:11:39.240
there's an ongoing endemic
of regular diseases

00:11:39.240 --> 00:11:41.020
that need to be addressed

00:11:42.030 --> 00:11:44.280
and that the population will want

00:11:44.280 --> 00:11:46.130
people like me to keep talking about.

00:11:47.778 --> 00:11:50.520
- All right, thank you,
Paramount Chief Farley.

00:11:50.520 --> 00:11:53.190
And so we'll bring it back to Dr. Dahn.

00:11:53.190 --> 00:11:56.310
What was the most significant
challenge you faced

00:11:56.310 --> 00:11:59.490
during the Ebola crisis in your country?

00:11:59.490 --> 00:12:00.940
And how did you respond?

00:12:02.699 --> 00:12:06.116
- Initially, coordinating to our partners

00:12:11.378 --> 00:12:16.378
to align to and respond to
the emergency was challenging.

00:12:18.060 --> 00:12:23.060
We had an emergency and
everyone was willing to help.

00:12:25.800 --> 00:12:30.800
So we had a lot of influx of partners,

00:12:30.900 --> 00:12:33.150
both donors and implementing partners,

00:12:33.150 --> 00:12:34.413
coming in to help.

00:12:35.850 --> 00:12:37.980
We were having coordination meetings,

00:12:37.980 --> 00:12:41.820
but from the initial stages,

00:12:41.820 --> 00:12:45.453
those coordination meetings
were not very efficient,

00:12:46.740 --> 00:12:51.740
and so partners were like parachuting,

00:12:52.200 --> 00:12:54.820
going, and doing things on their own

00:12:56.070 --> 00:13:00.243
until we were able to regroup,

00:13:02.460 --> 00:13:04.860
design a system that brought in

00:13:04.860 --> 00:13:07.473
the Incident Management System,

00:13:08.940 --> 00:13:12.123
established Emergency Operation Centers,

00:13:13.920 --> 00:13:16.800
mapped out the partners,

00:13:16.800 --> 00:13:19.000
and divided them geographically

00:13:19.980 --> 00:13:22.990
to support specific
things within the counties

00:13:23.970 --> 00:13:26.490
and help to build a county health capacity

00:13:26.490 --> 00:13:29.583
before we were able to get it straight.

00:13:34.783 --> 00:13:36.963
- All right, thank you, Dr. Dahn.

00:13:37.920 --> 00:13:39.780
And Dr. Thiam,

00:13:39.780 --> 00:13:42.720
what was the most significant
challenge you faced

00:13:42.720 --> 00:13:46.290
during the Ebola crisis
when you were in Guinea?

00:13:46.290 --> 00:13:48.003
And how did you respond?

00:13:53.210 --> 00:13:57.377
(Sylla Thiam speaking in French)

00:16:02.670 --> 00:16:03.870
- Thank you, Dr. Thiam.

00:16:03.870 --> 00:16:06.840
And Paramount Chief Farley,

00:16:06.840 --> 00:16:10.200
what would you say was the
most significant challenge

00:16:10.200 --> 00:16:12.923
that you faced on Ebola in Sierra Leone?

00:16:15.722 --> 00:16:16.650
- Okay, there are several issues,

00:16:16.650 --> 00:16:19.920
but I think they all
center around the fact that

00:16:19.920 --> 00:16:24.920
Ebola was the first really
major modern health crisis

00:16:25.800 --> 00:16:27.543
that the country had experienced.

00:16:28.680 --> 00:16:33.680
And so the first thing was
the very negative reaction

00:16:33.750 --> 00:16:35.760
of our local communities.

00:16:35.760 --> 00:16:37.080
We're a little bit fortunate

00:16:37.080 --> 00:16:39.570
that it started in the rural areas,

00:16:39.570 --> 00:16:42.543
and by the time it had
gotten to the urban areas,

00:16:45.360 --> 00:16:46.800
the situation was still bad,

00:16:46.800 --> 00:16:49.800
but at least the government

00:16:49.800 --> 00:16:52.260
had had some experience
with what was happening.

00:16:52.260 --> 00:16:55.050
But the major issue there was a stigma

00:16:55.050 --> 00:16:58.263
associated with the disease.

00:16:59.460 --> 00:17:01.960
When a community suddenly starts seeing

00:17:03.060 --> 00:17:07.020
there are several members
of their community

00:17:07.020 --> 00:17:09.543
going to hospital and dying consistently,

00:17:10.920 --> 00:17:14.970
and when they saw ambulances
come into community,

00:17:14.970 --> 00:17:18.150
they will usually, the
youth will gather up

00:17:18.150 --> 00:17:21.810
and throw stones at the ambulances

00:17:21.810 --> 00:17:26.810
and maybe not even accept
those health initiatives.

00:17:28.740 --> 00:17:33.430
Also because of sick stigma
associated with the disease

00:17:34.650 --> 00:17:37.590
and high fatality that was involved

00:17:37.590 --> 00:17:42.480
in the disease, because of a
very poor healthcare system,

00:17:42.480 --> 00:17:47.250
when you had Ebola it was
basically a sentence of death.

00:17:47.250 --> 00:17:49.950
And so people used to hide the fact

00:17:49.950 --> 00:17:51.753
that they had Ebola at all,

00:17:52.950 --> 00:17:56.100
and that obviously helped
in its transmission.

00:17:56.100 --> 00:17:58.360
There was also the issue of

00:18:00.570 --> 00:18:03.060
people wanting,

00:18:03.060 --> 00:18:08.060
our culture dictates that we
have a very caring community.

00:18:11.070 --> 00:18:14.913
Usually people usually visit sick people,

00:18:15.810 --> 00:18:18.693
and especially when somebody dies,

00:18:20.130 --> 00:18:22.020
a large number of people in communities

00:18:22.020 --> 00:18:26.610
usually attend to that person.

00:18:26.610 --> 00:18:29.313
And obviously in the case of Ebola,

00:18:30.480 --> 00:18:33.300
people who got in contact with somebody

00:18:33.300 --> 00:18:36.490
who even died from Ebola
or got sick from Ebola

00:18:37.585 --> 00:18:39.510
will easily get the disease.

00:18:39.510 --> 00:18:44.510
So it became almost a
hopeless situation where

00:18:45.780 --> 00:18:49.980
if they say you have Ebola
or if somebody got sick,

00:18:49.980 --> 00:18:52.920
he will hide the fact that he was sick.

00:18:52.920 --> 00:18:54.780
Even if he was just he or she

00:18:54.780 --> 00:18:57.480
was just sick with malaria or typhoid

00:18:57.480 --> 00:19:00.693
or some really perfectly curable disease,

00:19:01.590 --> 00:19:03.510
they will deny that
they have that disease,

00:19:03.510 --> 00:19:07.800
because once they were told,

00:19:07.800 --> 00:19:11.250
the authorities knew that they had Ebola,

00:19:11.250 --> 00:19:13.590
they'll be taken to an Ebola center

00:19:13.590 --> 00:19:17.013
where they'll invariably never return.

00:19:17.880 --> 00:19:21.600
It got a little bit
better as time went on,

00:19:21.600 --> 00:19:24.210
when we had better facilities.

00:19:24.210 --> 00:19:26.400
- Sorry, Paramount Chief
Farley, I have to interrupt.

00:19:26.400 --> 00:19:27.993
You got 10 seconds left.

00:19:28.860 --> 00:19:30.600
- Okay, it got a little bit better,

00:19:30.600 --> 00:19:34.680
but that was the main issue
that we had to deal with.

00:19:34.680 --> 00:19:36.150
Then of course we had to deal with

00:19:36.150 --> 00:19:39.420
the poor governance
structure that we have.

00:19:39.420 --> 00:19:41.830
But I'll address that later on.

00:19:41.830 --> 00:19:44.040
- Okay, thank you, Paramount Chief Farley.

00:19:44.040 --> 00:19:45.780
So we're moving on to the next round

00:19:45.780 --> 00:19:49.680
where each panelist will
have one minute to respond.

00:19:49.680 --> 00:19:51.930
So if a panelist is responding

00:19:51.930 --> 00:19:55.470
and another panelist likes
the points that they made,

00:19:55.470 --> 00:19:58.500
they can raise two fingers,
such as I'm demonstrating,

00:19:58.500 --> 00:20:01.950
and they can add on to that
person's point for 20 seconds.

00:20:01.950 --> 00:20:04.920
Or if you can raise a full hand to object,

00:20:04.920 --> 00:20:06.840
if you wanna give a dissenting view.

00:20:06.840 --> 00:20:08.910
You'll have 30 seconds to give that view,

00:20:08.910 --> 00:20:11.400
and then the original panelists will have

00:20:11.400 --> 00:20:13.710
30 seconds to respond to that view.

00:20:13.710 --> 00:20:16.380
And yes, I am timing it on my timer.

00:20:16.380 --> 00:20:18.783
So we will be begin with Dr. Dahn.

00:20:19.890 --> 00:20:22.320
You also have the unique experience

00:20:22.320 --> 00:20:25.410
of working inside a Ministry of Health.

00:20:25.410 --> 00:20:28.140
And what do you feel were the challenges

00:20:28.140 --> 00:20:31.410
of integrating those NGO resources

00:20:31.410 --> 00:20:35.013
in a government response and
how can that be improved?

00:20:38.850 --> 00:20:42.763
- Is the question related to
the Ebola or just the overall?

00:20:43.695 --> 00:20:46.560
- It's related to Ebola for now.

00:20:46.560 --> 00:20:50.013
- Okay, okay, so yes, thank you.

00:20:54.420 --> 00:20:59.420
Normally partners, both donor partners

00:20:59.520 --> 00:21:04.053
and implementing partners,
will stress accountability,

00:21:05.940 --> 00:21:10.023
efficiency, and accountability.

00:21:12.720 --> 00:21:14.853
Working with the government,

00:21:16.710 --> 00:21:21.363
you'll realize that accountability
is a one-way street.

00:21:23.350 --> 00:21:28.350
Okay, so I was saying
normally within global health

00:21:29.790 --> 00:21:33.870
we all stress accountability
and efficiency,

00:21:33.870 --> 00:21:35.643
effectiveness and efficiency.

00:21:37.800 --> 00:21:39.483
When you work in the government,

00:21:41.290 --> 00:21:43.890
you realize that accountability
is a one-way street.

00:21:45.120 --> 00:21:48.810
Donor partners tend to hold
governments accountable

00:21:48.810 --> 00:21:51.580
for resources that are provided for them

00:21:52.560 --> 00:21:53.793
from their countries,

00:21:57.931 --> 00:22:01.500
but the country, the recipient,

00:22:01.500 --> 00:22:05.910
does not have the same opportunity

00:22:05.910 --> 00:22:10.910
to ask for accountability
in the other direction.

00:22:12.120 --> 00:22:15.463
So you'll realize that, for example,

00:22:15.463 --> 00:22:20.010
a donor gives an NGO maybe $10 million.

00:22:20.010 --> 00:22:23.070
- Oh, Dr. Dahn, could
you wrap up the example

00:22:23.070 --> 00:22:26.040
in just 10 seconds, please?

00:22:26.040 --> 00:22:27.420
- Okay, a donor gives

00:22:27.420 --> 00:22:31.563
an NGO $10 million to support services.

00:22:33.390 --> 00:22:35.730
You ask for detailed services.

00:22:35.730 --> 00:22:39.633
A detailed breakdown of that
budget is difficult to get.

00:22:40.560 --> 00:22:44.850
And so it becomes difficult
to align that resource

00:22:44.850 --> 00:22:46.950
to resources that you already have

00:22:46.950 --> 00:22:49.920
to be able to complement one another.

00:22:49.920 --> 00:22:51.360
Thank you.

00:22:51.360 --> 00:22:53.310
- Thank you, Dr. Dahn.

00:22:53.310 --> 00:22:55.830
And for Paramount Chief Farley,

00:22:55.830 --> 00:22:58.740
can you please tell us
about the interactions

00:22:58.740 --> 00:23:01.470
between emergency responders

00:23:01.470 --> 00:23:03.780
and the local communities, you know,

00:23:03.780 --> 00:23:06.690
such as the burial teams,
healthcare workers,

00:23:06.690 --> 00:23:08.240
that you had mentioned earlier?

00:23:10.110 --> 00:23:11.510
- It started out really bad.

00:23:13.350 --> 00:23:17.530
As I said, the first time
I actually noticed that

00:23:18.600 --> 00:23:20.380
there was a problem

00:23:23.909 --> 00:23:27.409
in the country, or at least in our region.

00:23:27.409 --> 00:23:32.409
We had just come back
from (voice muffling).

00:23:42.060 --> 00:23:44.790
- [Shannon] Farley, we're
having a trouble hearing you.

00:23:44.790 --> 00:23:46.680
If you could speak a
little louder, please.

00:23:46.680 --> 00:23:47.880
We're having a little trouble hearing you.

00:23:47.880 --> 00:23:48.713
Thank you.

00:23:50.123 --> 00:23:53.490
- All right, and I said the first time

00:23:53.490 --> 00:23:56.940
I noticed that we had a
problem in the country

00:23:56.940 --> 00:24:01.530
was when we were traveling from a meeting

00:24:01.530 --> 00:24:05.940
of paramount chiefs in
Kailahun district at night,

00:24:05.940 --> 00:24:09.660
and I saw a huge crowd on the roadside

00:24:09.660 --> 00:24:11.583
attacking an ambulance.

00:24:12.750 --> 00:24:15.930
And I had to come down and
I asked what was going on.

00:24:15.930 --> 00:24:20.290
I found out that they said
the ambulance people had said

00:24:23.978 --> 00:24:25.390
they had brought in a corpse

00:24:26.341 --> 00:24:28.710
who died from a new disease

00:24:28.710 --> 00:24:30.390
and that there were under instructions

00:24:30.390 --> 00:24:34.350
not to let the people handle that corpse.

00:24:34.350 --> 00:24:37.233
And so there was a huge
hue and cry about that.

00:24:39.330 --> 00:24:40.743
But that was the start.

00:24:41.730 --> 00:24:42.960
But then unfortunately,

00:24:42.960 --> 00:24:46.650
or maybe, I don't know, providentially,

00:24:46.650 --> 00:24:49.500
in that same chiefdom,
not very long after that,

00:24:49.500 --> 00:24:51.350
the wife of the paramount chief died.

00:24:52.603 --> 00:24:56.170
And so there was a much better thrust

00:24:57.417 --> 00:25:01.260
of wanting to handle this issue together

00:25:01.260 --> 00:25:04.660
so that by the end of the epidemic

00:25:05.736 --> 00:25:08.340
there was a very, very good relationship

00:25:08.340 --> 00:25:12.840
between the community, the
medical care providers,

00:25:12.840 --> 00:25:17.840
and the traditional leaders like us.

00:25:18.900 --> 00:25:21.120
So it started out very badly,

00:25:21.120 --> 00:25:24.771
but over a period of time we ended up

00:25:24.771 --> 00:25:26.970
with a fairly good relationship,

00:25:26.970 --> 00:25:29.400
and it's a good lesson that we'll learn

00:25:29.400 --> 00:25:30.783
for future interventions.

00:25:33.420 --> 00:25:35.249
- All right, thank you.

00:25:35.249 --> 00:25:37.560
Dr. Dahn, can we go back for you

00:25:37.560 --> 00:25:39.090
to finish your thought, please?

00:25:39.090 --> 00:25:44.090
In terms of, so what were the...

00:25:44.310 --> 00:25:46.830
Oh, I'm sorry, to the original question,

00:25:46.830 --> 00:25:50.250
what were the challenges of
integrating those NGO resources

00:25:50.250 --> 00:25:53.700
into the government response
and how these can be improved?

00:25:53.700 --> 00:25:55.893
So, you left off at accountability.

00:25:56.910 --> 00:26:01.910
- Yes, so I was saying it is difficult to,

00:26:02.280 --> 00:26:03.780
especially for implementing partners

00:26:03.780 --> 00:26:05.673
to share detailed budgets,

00:26:07.200 --> 00:26:11.730
and so aligning those budgets

00:26:11.730 --> 00:26:16.470
to what you already have,
resources that you have,

00:26:16.470 --> 00:26:19.530
to ensure that they complement
one another is difficult.

00:26:19.530 --> 00:26:24.530
Take for example, quite
recently there was an RFA.

00:26:24.960 --> 00:26:29.960
We decided to join with
an NGO partner to apply.

00:26:34.770 --> 00:26:38.040
The RFA, one of the terms

00:26:38.040 --> 00:26:40.593
is to strengthen local health systems.

00:26:41.460 --> 00:26:46.460
And so in that RFA 50% of the budget

00:26:47.340 --> 00:26:49.773
should go to local institutions.

00:26:51.090 --> 00:26:53.520
The international partner was the one

00:26:53.520 --> 00:26:55.143
priming, we agreed to that.

00:26:58.470 --> 00:27:00.780
They broke down the budget

00:27:00.780 --> 00:27:04.440
and gave us about 5.4

00:27:04.440 --> 00:27:09.440
out of $24 million for over five years.

00:27:10.230 --> 00:27:13.360
We did a detailed budget for our portion

00:27:15.210 --> 00:27:17.797
of the $24 million, which is the 5.4,

00:27:18.720 --> 00:27:21.090
but we just couldn't get
them to give us the details.

00:27:21.090 --> 00:27:24.363
And we were partners on the
table designing a project.

00:27:26.640 --> 00:27:29.250
And that is, even, it is not the only one,

00:27:29.250 --> 00:27:30.963
but it's been happening.

00:27:33.060 --> 00:27:37.110
I've noticed a difference
with training institutions

00:27:37.110 --> 00:27:38.613
and also the health sector.

00:27:39.540 --> 00:27:41.850
Most U.S. universities, for example,

00:27:41.850 --> 00:27:46.650
will tell you our overhead is 25%, 35%.

00:27:46.650 --> 00:27:49.500
So you know that, and when
you are designing the project,

00:27:49.500 --> 00:27:51.723
you take that into consideration,

00:27:52.830 --> 00:27:55.800
but all the U.S. NGOs

00:27:55.800 --> 00:27:58.443
will not tell you what their overhead is.

00:27:59.310 --> 00:28:02.043
Is not negotiated with their government.

00:28:03.510 --> 00:28:05.010
It's outside of it,

00:28:05.010 --> 00:28:08.130
so you really don't know
what their overhead is.

00:28:08.130 --> 00:28:09.480
And then at the end of the day,

00:28:09.480 --> 00:28:12.720
you realize that what
actually goes to the project

00:28:12.720 --> 00:28:15.030
is by far less than what it is.

00:28:15.030 --> 00:28:16.830
And that has implications for government,

00:28:16.830 --> 00:28:21.830
because when you sign
a $50 million project,

00:28:22.380 --> 00:28:23.970
for example, in Liberia,

00:28:23.970 --> 00:28:28.263
Liberians want to see $50
million worth of projects,

00:28:29.490 --> 00:28:31.740
in terms of implementation.

00:28:31.740 --> 00:28:33.120
And so when they don't see that,

00:28:33.120 --> 00:28:36.057
they feel that the money has
been (voice cutting out).

00:28:38.040 --> 00:28:41.100
Government is abusing the
money and things like that.

00:28:41.100 --> 00:28:44.293
But many times those monies
don't even come to the country.

00:28:44.293 --> 00:28:46.380
A good portion of it don't
even come to the country,

00:28:46.380 --> 00:28:48.760
and you really don't see how much

00:28:49.860 --> 00:28:53.250
to really say this is the amount

00:28:53.250 --> 00:28:56.130
that actually went with
program and services

00:28:56.130 --> 00:28:58.920
and this went into overhead for others.

00:28:58.920 --> 00:29:00.690
And that I've seen the difference also

00:29:00.690 --> 00:29:03.210
with some of the U.S.
training institutions

00:29:03.210 --> 00:29:04.470
that they'll be transparent,

00:29:04.470 --> 00:29:06.450
and we'll all work the budget together.

00:29:06.450 --> 00:29:09.180
Ask (voice cutting out) NGO partners that

00:29:09.180 --> 00:29:12.510
you really don't know how it goes.

00:29:12.510 --> 00:29:13.343
Thank you.

00:29:14.610 --> 00:29:15.810
- Thank you, Dr. Dahn.

00:29:15.810 --> 00:29:19.380
I see Paramount Chief
Farley has two fingers up,

00:29:19.380 --> 00:29:20.340
so please add on.

00:29:20.340 --> 00:29:22.110
You have 20 seconds.

00:29:22.110 --> 00:29:24.803
- Yes, I'll just add that

00:29:24.803 --> 00:29:27.450
there's an accountability issue also

00:29:27.450 --> 00:29:30.263
on the part of our national governments.

00:29:30.263 --> 00:29:34.830
I govern a local
(indistinct) local government

00:29:34.830 --> 00:29:39.830
that used to exist even
before the colonial era,

00:29:39.900 --> 00:29:41.820
and our people implicitly trust

00:29:41.820 --> 00:29:43.530
their paramount chiefs significantly,

00:29:43.530 --> 00:29:45.690
much more so than central government.

00:29:45.690 --> 00:29:49.140
But even when we get donations
to our central governments,

00:29:49.140 --> 00:29:51.887
very little of it triggers

00:29:51.887 --> 00:29:55.500
into our local communities,
into chiefdoms,

00:29:55.500 --> 00:29:57.720
like we have, for instance.

00:29:57.720 --> 00:30:00.420
And so it's a double whammy.

00:30:00.420 --> 00:30:04.950
Dr. Dahn has a problem with
accountability from NGOs.

00:30:04.950 --> 00:30:06.360
We have a problem,

00:30:06.360 --> 00:30:09.810
but NGOs still give a little
bit to the central government.

00:30:09.810 --> 00:30:10.740
But even that little bit,

00:30:10.740 --> 00:30:13.920
the central government they
give the central government,

00:30:13.920 --> 00:30:15.870
does not get to the communities

00:30:15.870 --> 00:30:19.971
which actually get to implement
things that actually address

00:30:19.971 --> 00:30:23.010
the diseases, the problems
we have in our communities.

00:30:23.010 --> 00:30:24.967
So I just wanted to address that.

00:30:26.280 --> 00:30:28.233
- Thank you, Paramount Chief Farley.

00:30:29.220 --> 00:30:32.790
And to follow up finally
with the Dr. Thiam.

00:30:32.790 --> 00:30:35.220
So you also have the unique experience

00:30:35.220 --> 00:30:37.380
of working inside a Ministry of Health

00:30:37.380 --> 00:30:40.530
and then working for a
major health NGO, Amref.

00:30:40.530 --> 00:30:42.960
So what do you feel are the challenges

00:30:42.960 --> 00:30:45.494
of integrating NGO resources

00:30:45.494 --> 00:30:48.660
in this type of government
coordinated response?

00:30:48.660 --> 00:30:50.313
How can that be improved?

00:30:52.579 --> 00:30:56.662
(Sylla Thiam speaking in French)

00:32:22.809 --> 00:32:25.080
- Thank you, Dr. Thiam.

00:32:25.080 --> 00:32:28.980
And thinking now, going
back to the very beginning

00:32:28.980 --> 00:32:30.540
of the Ebola epidemic

00:32:30.540 --> 00:32:33.090
and the misconceptions around it,

00:32:33.090 --> 00:32:36.630
such as stigma, local culture or customs,

00:32:36.630 --> 00:32:40.800
or the involvement of local
communities in some regions,

00:32:40.800 --> 00:32:44.730
as a result, distrust developed
between those communities

00:32:44.730 --> 00:32:47.490
and those seeking to control the epidemic.

00:32:47.490 --> 00:32:50.250
So, after working in
these different regions

00:32:50.250 --> 00:32:54.570
and your position as
regional director of Amref,

00:32:54.570 --> 00:32:55.920
what have you learned

00:32:55.920 --> 00:32:59.760
and how can we prevent
mistrust between communities

00:32:59.760 --> 00:33:01.700
and public health responders?

00:33:03.523 --> 00:33:07.440
(Sylla Thiam speaking French)

00:35:47.710 --> 00:35:49.606
- Thank you, Dr. Thiam.

00:35:49.606 --> 00:35:52.590
And Paramount Chief Farley,

00:35:52.590 --> 00:35:55.350
thinking about the
international responses,

00:35:55.350 --> 00:35:58.200
for example, MSF or Western governments

00:35:58.200 --> 00:36:00.300
that are often put in a spotlight

00:36:00.300 --> 00:36:04.500
when dealing with a global
health crisis such as Ebola,

00:36:04.500 --> 00:36:07.320
how has your work with
other paramount chiefs

00:36:07.320 --> 00:36:10.409
demonstrates that local communities

00:36:10.409 --> 00:36:12.750
are capable of meeting these challenges?

00:36:12.750 --> 00:36:15.480
So how can international organizations

00:36:15.480 --> 00:36:18.780
or other governments better
support local communities

00:36:18.780 --> 00:36:21.453
to address future emergencies?

00:36:22.807 --> 00:36:25.770
- Deal with paramount chiefs directly.

00:36:25.770 --> 00:36:27.090
Most of our communities

00:36:27.090 --> 00:36:28.950
have traditional governance structures

00:36:28.950 --> 00:36:31.740
that the communities trust

00:36:31.740 --> 00:36:34.263
a whole lot more than
the national government.

00:36:35.220 --> 00:36:37.410
Most of our national governments

00:36:37.410 --> 00:36:41.581
are essentially inheritance of a mantle

00:36:41.581 --> 00:36:43.593
of the colonial authorities.

00:36:44.670 --> 00:36:47.853
And unfortunately in a
lot of African countries,

00:36:50.160 --> 00:36:51.750
especially in the rural areas,

00:36:51.750 --> 00:36:54.993
or in the vast majority of the country,

00:36:55.950 --> 00:36:58.680
the people in governance in a major,

00:36:58.680 --> 00:37:03.680
at the national level
basically take an attitude

00:37:05.430 --> 00:37:10.430
of a colonialist towards the rural areas.

00:37:10.650 --> 00:37:13.323
And so, like in Sierra Leone,

00:37:14.160 --> 00:37:17.070
things are very highly centralized.

00:37:17.070 --> 00:37:19.320
Most of our traditional structures

00:37:19.320 --> 00:37:21.573
have been weakened significantly.

00:37:23.010 --> 00:37:25.650
And since our productive capacity

00:37:25.650 --> 00:37:27.993
has been reduced also significantly,

00:37:29.130 --> 00:37:32.340
to a large extent, we
depend to a large extent

00:37:32.340 --> 00:37:34.770
on funds coming in from outside.

00:37:34.770 --> 00:37:38.970
Now, a lot of these funds go
directly to the government,

00:37:38.970 --> 00:37:42.660
but a lot of it also goes to NGOs.

00:37:42.660 --> 00:37:45.150
None of it goes to paramount chief

00:37:45.150 --> 00:37:47.460
and local leaders directly.

00:37:47.460 --> 00:37:51.630
And yet we are the
people that people trust

00:37:51.630 --> 00:37:53.620
more than anybody else to deliver

00:37:55.350 --> 00:37:58.740
the care that they need.

00:37:58.740 --> 00:38:00.483
So my suggestion,

00:38:02.250 --> 00:38:05.980
one way we could think
about it is obviously

00:38:07.200 --> 00:38:09.420
get our NGOs very accountable,

00:38:09.420 --> 00:38:13.120
but also give them instructions

00:38:14.070 --> 00:38:15.820
and find a way to monitor whether

00:38:17.400 --> 00:38:19.800
they have had direct relationships

00:38:19.800 --> 00:38:22.533
with traditional leaders
of those communities.

00:38:23.520 --> 00:38:27.180
And there are all sorts of ways

00:38:27.180 --> 00:38:29.490
that we could structure that.

00:38:29.490 --> 00:38:33.330
But on a large, like on a broad basis,

00:38:33.330 --> 00:38:37.230
I think that probably
is the best way to go.

00:38:37.230 --> 00:38:41.340
International countries or organizations

00:38:41.340 --> 00:38:44.280
and NGOs when they come, yes,

00:38:44.280 --> 00:38:47.130
you need some permission from
the government to operate,

00:38:47.130 --> 00:38:49.620
but make sure that most of your resources

00:38:49.620 --> 00:38:52.890
are directed towards direct interactions

00:38:52.890 --> 00:38:55.500
with your traditional
leaders whom the communities

00:38:55.500 --> 00:38:57.750
respond to a lot better

00:38:57.750 --> 00:39:00.450
and who are a lot more
accountable to the communities.

00:39:01.440 --> 00:39:03.270
- Thank you, Paramount Chief Farley.

00:39:03.270 --> 00:39:07.320
And Dr. Dahn, we wanna
wrap up this section.

00:39:07.320 --> 00:39:09.780
Can you please tell us
about the lessons learned

00:39:09.780 --> 00:39:12.150
from Liberia's experience with Ebola

00:39:12.150 --> 00:39:16.530
and how that could have been
informed for this current

00:39:16.530 --> 00:39:18.543
or recent outbreak with COVID?

00:39:21.300 --> 00:39:22.203
- Thank you.

00:39:23.310 --> 00:39:26.400
The systems we put in place

00:39:26.400 --> 00:39:31.400
for the Ebola outbreak help
us with the COVID outbreak.

00:39:36.661 --> 00:39:37.650
It was during the Ebola time

00:39:37.650 --> 00:39:42.423
that we established
the emergency, the IMS,

00:39:43.590 --> 00:39:47.970
Emergency Management System,
the Incident Management System,

00:39:47.970 --> 00:39:52.093
and then established these
EOCs in the 15 counties,

00:39:53.790 --> 00:39:55.953
Emergency Operation Centers.

00:39:57.060 --> 00:40:01.470
We also put in place a
full surveillance system

00:40:01.470 --> 00:40:03.630
from community-based events

00:40:03.630 --> 00:40:07.560
reporting to real disease surveillance,

00:40:07.560 --> 00:40:10.621
active surveillance system
within the communities

00:40:10.621 --> 00:40:12.900
all the way to the central level

00:40:12.900 --> 00:40:15.603
that is reported within 24, 48 hours.

00:40:17.400 --> 00:40:20.610
So, those systems, the IMS,

00:40:20.610 --> 00:40:24.033
is normally activated
when there is an outbreak.

00:40:25.830 --> 00:40:28.230
And then after the outbreak
(voice cutting out).

00:40:31.889 --> 00:40:35.880
The EOCs continue to
function within the counties,

00:40:35.880 --> 00:40:39.150
because we will always have some outbreaks

00:40:39.150 --> 00:40:41.820
that we can manage normally.

00:40:41.820 --> 00:40:44.280
Measle outbreak, for
example, yellow fever,

00:40:44.280 --> 00:40:45.840
those things continue.

00:40:45.840 --> 00:40:50.840
So the EOC continue to
function at the county level.

00:40:50.880 --> 00:40:54.720
So with the outbreak of the COVID,

00:40:54.720 --> 00:40:58.203
the IMS was reactivated quickly.

00:40:59.790 --> 00:41:02.700
All the EOCs, they are functional,

00:41:02.700 --> 00:41:06.783
and so we were able to intervene quickly.

00:41:08.250 --> 00:41:13.250
Now we also had a few
challenges with that also.

00:41:14.070 --> 00:41:18.303
The central level,
which is the IMS system,

00:41:19.890 --> 00:41:22.980
we established during the...

00:41:22.980 --> 00:41:25.350
After the Ebola, we established

00:41:25.350 --> 00:41:27.630
the National Public Health Institutes,

00:41:27.630 --> 00:41:30.120
and the institute is responsible

00:41:30.120 --> 00:41:34.863
for disease surveillance
and emergency response.

00:41:35.790 --> 00:41:38.160
The definition of that emergency response,

00:41:38.160 --> 00:41:41.580
because we know that at some point

00:41:41.580 --> 00:41:44.700
the routine health services
had to come in place,

00:41:44.700 --> 00:41:49.683
especially for case management
within the emergency system.

00:41:51.120 --> 00:41:54.780
So there was this struggle
between the central ministry

00:41:54.780 --> 00:41:57.120
and the National Public Health Institute

00:41:57.120 --> 00:42:01.403
as to who provides the leadership
for the COVID response.

00:42:02.640 --> 00:42:06.727
But fortunately for us at
the decentralized level,

00:42:06.727 --> 00:42:08.810
the EOCs were functional,

00:42:09.731 --> 00:42:12.690
so they were able to intervene quickly.

00:42:12.690 --> 00:42:17.277
And then WHO actually
supported those of us

00:42:17.277 --> 00:42:20.100
who were in country and had the experience

00:42:20.100 --> 00:42:21.800
of managing the emergency to work

00:42:21.800 --> 00:42:24.810
at the decentralized level, some of us,

00:42:24.810 --> 00:42:27.690
especially where we had the outbreaks

00:42:27.690 --> 00:42:30.060
to be able to curtail the disease.

00:42:30.060 --> 00:42:32.190
So the system actually helped us,

00:42:32.190 --> 00:42:34.260
and then the impact was not as much

00:42:34.260 --> 00:42:37.083
as we saw in other countries.

00:42:38.740 --> 00:42:41.190
- Okay, thank you, Dr. Dahn.

00:42:41.190 --> 00:42:43.500
So, we wanna move to this last round

00:42:43.500 --> 00:42:46.233
before we open it up for Q&amp;A

00:42:46.233 --> 00:42:48.360
to give the panelists a chance

00:42:48.360 --> 00:42:51.150
to respond to the same question.

00:42:51.150 --> 00:42:54.930
So each panelist again will
have 60 seconds to respond.

00:42:54.930 --> 00:42:58.740
You can raise two fingers to
agree and add on for 20 seconds

00:42:58.740 --> 00:43:02.280
or a full hand to dissent and
then add on for 30 seconds.

00:43:02.280 --> 00:43:04.560
And then the original panel member

00:43:04.560 --> 00:43:07.260
will have 30 seconds to respond.

00:43:07.260 --> 00:43:11.730
So, how can universal
access to primary care,

00:43:11.730 --> 00:43:13.920
healthcare, in low income countries

00:43:13.920 --> 00:43:16.170
improve global health security?

00:43:16.170 --> 00:43:19.713
What do donors in Western-based
agencies need to hear?

00:43:21.330 --> 00:43:22.893
We'll start with Dr. Thiam.

00:43:26.640 --> 00:43:29.187
Dr. Thiam, you're on mute.

00:43:29.187 --> 00:43:32.433
- Oh, okay, not me, right?
- No, Dr. Thiam.

00:43:34.140 --> 00:43:34.983
- Okay.

00:43:35.991 --> 00:43:39.824
(Sylla Thiam speaking French)

00:45:11.231 --> 00:45:13.380
- Thank you, Dr. Thiam.

00:45:13.380 --> 00:45:14.823
Paramount Chief Farley.

00:45:17.400 --> 00:45:18.640
- Thank you.

00:45:18.640 --> 00:45:21.420
First of all, we have to realize that

00:45:21.420 --> 00:45:25.863
we live in a much smaller
world now than in the past.

00:45:30.600 --> 00:45:33.843
Some diseases aren't quite
as infectious as others,

00:45:35.220 --> 00:45:40.220
but they are nevertheless
endemic to the local communities.

00:45:41.850 --> 00:45:46.850
But if a community is used to
having very poor healthcare

00:45:50.520 --> 00:45:54.450
to even take care of malaria and typhoid

00:45:54.450 --> 00:45:55.865
and things like that,

00:45:55.865 --> 00:45:58.713
which a lot of people still die of,

00:45:59.940 --> 00:46:04.940
not because they don't
know what the diseases is.

00:46:05.460 --> 00:46:07.890
But it gets to a point
where they have to choose

00:46:07.890 --> 00:46:12.890
between buying food to
eat or buy medicine.

00:46:13.620 --> 00:46:16.230
And invariably they'll buy food

00:46:16.230 --> 00:46:19.260
or keep buying food until
the disease gets so chronic

00:46:19.260 --> 00:46:22.620
that it becomes incurable.

00:46:22.620 --> 00:46:27.390
Now, if you have a
community that is inured

00:46:27.390 --> 00:46:29.370
to something like that,

00:46:29.370 --> 00:46:33.240
when you actually have something
that can be transmitted,

00:46:33.240 --> 00:46:35.610
that becomes a threat to Western nations,

00:46:35.610 --> 00:46:38.940
like Ebola, people don't
take it very seriously

00:46:38.940 --> 00:46:41.400
because they already die every day.

00:46:41.400 --> 00:46:43.770
And so they have a very low expectation

00:46:43.770 --> 00:46:45.930
of healthcare delivery,

00:46:45.930 --> 00:46:49.110
and therefore, they
are not very responsive

00:46:49.110 --> 00:46:54.110
to diseases that the West
will take a lot more.

00:46:56.160 --> 00:46:57.630
I'll give you an example.

00:46:57.630 --> 00:46:59.880
Like, in the case of COVID,

00:46:59.880 --> 00:47:04.150
I'll tell you that I don't
know by what miracle COVID

00:47:05.190 --> 00:47:09.030
wasn't quite as infectious around here

00:47:09.030 --> 00:47:10.350
as it was in the West,

00:47:10.350 --> 00:47:12.280
but if it were, it'll have killed

00:47:13.613 --> 00:47:15.363
a whole lot more people than Ebola.

00:47:16.350 --> 00:47:19.447
But it's the same phenomenon that people

00:47:19.447 --> 00:47:23.400
have been used to having
very low expectation

00:47:23.400 --> 00:47:25.470
of healthcare delivery system.

00:47:25.470 --> 00:47:29.730
And so when infectious diseases come

00:47:29.730 --> 00:47:34.730
that could affect the West
because of the travel,

00:47:35.129 --> 00:47:38.940
the ease of travel around the world,

00:47:38.940 --> 00:47:40.230
that becomes the threat.

00:47:40.230 --> 00:47:43.230
So we all have an interest in improving

00:47:43.230 --> 00:47:46.500
that primary healthcare
within our communities.

00:47:46.500 --> 00:47:50.593
And my final point is whatever you do,

00:47:50.593 --> 00:47:54.930
start at the local governance level.

00:47:54.930 --> 00:47:56.610
A lot of it at the national level,

00:47:56.610 --> 00:47:59.073
never trickles down there,

00:48:00.240 --> 00:48:04.020
but I think I'll talk about
it more down the line.

00:48:05.040 --> 00:48:07.350
- Okay, thank you, Paramount Chief Farley.

00:48:07.350 --> 00:48:08.223
Dr. Dahn.

00:48:09.480 --> 00:48:10.623
- Okay, thank you.

00:48:11.580 --> 00:48:15.030
I will start by saying weak systems

00:48:15.030 --> 00:48:17.070
will continue to remain weak

00:48:17.070 --> 00:48:19.953
if the systems are not invested in.

00:48:21.150 --> 00:48:23.670
The first place is the government.

00:48:23.670 --> 00:48:27.510
Often we all look up to
the national government

00:48:27.510 --> 00:48:30.753
to invest in building systems.

00:48:32.520 --> 00:48:35.640
Now when you look at
the government's budget,

00:48:35.640 --> 00:48:39.450
if you take Liberia, for
example, that is a resource,

00:48:39.450 --> 00:48:41.970
the low income country

00:48:41.970 --> 00:48:43.710
and resources are challenging,

00:48:43.710 --> 00:48:48.710
you will realize that about
70 to 80% of the budget

00:48:51.507 --> 00:48:53.490
allocated to the health sector

00:48:53.490 --> 00:48:58.323
actually goes towards payment
of salaries to personnel.

00:48:59.880 --> 00:49:02.940
Now, the salaries also have challenges.

00:49:02.940 --> 00:49:06.330
They are salaries that
cannot meet the needs

00:49:06.330 --> 00:49:08.550
of the people who are doing the work.

00:49:08.550 --> 00:49:09.850
So that's the first thing.

00:49:11.520 --> 00:49:15.660
Then you are left with 28 to 30%

00:49:15.660 --> 00:49:19.920
of the amount to build all the systems

00:49:19.920 --> 00:49:21.213
that need to be in place,

00:49:22.860 --> 00:49:25.260
especially at the decentralized level

00:49:25.260 --> 00:49:29.763
where the actual services
are being delivered.

00:49:31.800 --> 00:49:36.800
That tells you that to
that it will be difficult.

00:49:38.010 --> 00:49:40.590
Now, many times we have partners

00:49:40.590 --> 00:49:43.773
who are willing to invest in helping,

00:49:44.790 --> 00:49:47.850
but they also tend to look at

00:49:47.850 --> 00:49:51.580
before they invest and gave
the government the funding

00:49:52.650 --> 00:49:57.650
to implement, they will
complain about the weak system,

00:49:58.983 --> 00:50:01.787
that they prefer to give the money

00:50:01.787 --> 00:50:04.950
to NGOs to implement.

00:50:04.950 --> 00:50:07.773
And so the government
system is never built.

00:50:08.700 --> 00:50:11.550
Now, NGOs will implement,

00:50:11.550 --> 00:50:15.243
and when the funding is
finished, withdrawal.

00:50:16.440 --> 00:50:19.950
When they withdraw the work is left back

00:50:19.950 --> 00:50:23.400
for the government to take on

00:50:23.400 --> 00:50:26.370
and the system is not there to take it on.

00:50:26.370 --> 00:50:29.610
So it's a virtual cycle.

00:50:29.610 --> 00:50:31.830
Now, within it, the NGO community, too,

00:50:31.830 --> 00:50:34.170
there are international communities

00:50:34.170 --> 00:50:38.370
and international NGOs
and there are local NGOs.

00:50:38.370 --> 00:50:41.910
Normally the international
NGOs are stronger

00:50:41.910 --> 00:50:44.370
than the local NGOs.

00:50:44.370 --> 00:50:46.350
So, most of the time the funding

00:50:46.350 --> 00:50:48.933
also goes to the international NGOs.

00:50:50.040 --> 00:50:52.170
And then when they have the funding,

00:50:52.170 --> 00:50:55.260
they recruit from within the system,

00:50:55.260 --> 00:50:58.083
often the government
system, to do the work.

00:50:59.070 --> 00:51:01.770
So, and then they pay better.

00:51:01.770 --> 00:51:05.373
So then the government system
is being depleted further.

00:51:06.300 --> 00:51:10.054
So the system is never really built

00:51:10.054 --> 00:51:14.523
for the government to be able
to take charge of the system.

00:51:15.540 --> 00:51:19.200
Now, government, too,
has its own problems.

00:51:19.200 --> 00:51:23.100
We have had an opportunity
to move from one government

00:51:23.100 --> 00:51:24.393
to another government,

00:51:25.320 --> 00:51:28.860
and you realize that some partners

00:51:28.860 --> 00:51:31.290
have helped to build some systems.

00:51:31.290 --> 00:51:34.653
If you take the financial
management system, for example,

00:51:35.772 --> 00:51:39.873
for Liberia, the health, the
Union Ministry of Health,

00:51:41.700 --> 00:51:43.650
during the previous administration,

00:51:43.650 --> 00:51:46.020
we had donor partners that help us

00:51:46.020 --> 00:51:48.450
to put in systems for
financial management,

00:51:48.450 --> 00:51:52.893
procurement, and the internal
audit, and the rest of it.

00:51:54.720 --> 00:51:59.643
Those systems function to a good extent.

00:52:00.870 --> 00:52:02.590
When you change government

00:52:04.080 --> 00:52:06.033
and another government comes in,

00:52:06.960 --> 00:52:10.350
then the other government,
instead of looking at the system,

00:52:10.350 --> 00:52:12.210
the new government looking at the system

00:52:12.210 --> 00:52:14.490
that existed and built on it,

00:52:14.490 --> 00:52:18.180
they start to do something
else new altogether.

00:52:18.180 --> 00:52:23.180
And so what was started
before never get to continue.

00:52:23.940 --> 00:52:26.850
And then the system continued to be weak.

00:52:26.850 --> 00:52:28.140
So it is both ways,

00:52:28.140 --> 00:52:31.620
and that need to be looked into carefully.

00:52:31.620 --> 00:52:33.480
The governments themselves,
maybe they don't,

00:52:33.480 --> 00:52:35.400
I don't know whether they understand

00:52:35.400 --> 00:52:37.190
building a regular system.

00:52:37.190 --> 00:52:42.190
It is more political driven
than actual technical work.

00:52:44.190 --> 00:52:46.890
And so we continue to
have this vicious cycle

00:52:46.890 --> 00:52:51.890
of growing small and taking
like three steps in front

00:52:53.160 --> 00:52:56.160
and 10 steps backwards,
once you change government.

00:52:56.160 --> 00:52:58.660
And so there's a need to
also really look at that.

00:52:59.820 --> 00:53:02.520
The government systems
need to be built properly.

00:53:02.520 --> 00:53:06.090
Government and partners work together

00:53:06.090 --> 00:53:09.540
to build the system so that
it will be sustainable.

00:53:09.540 --> 00:53:10.373
Thank you.

00:53:12.210 --> 00:53:13.077
- Thank you, Dr. Dahn.

00:53:13.077 --> 00:53:14.700
And I see two fingers.

00:53:14.700 --> 00:53:17.880
First from Paramount Chief
Farley and then Dr. Thiam.

00:53:17.880 --> 00:53:19.440
Chief Farley, yes.

00:53:19.440 --> 00:53:22.293
- Just a quick point to buttress
what Dr. Dahn is saying,

00:53:23.430 --> 00:53:27.860
and a solution to the problem that of

00:53:29.370 --> 00:53:32.250
changing the whole
system at national level,

00:53:32.250 --> 00:53:34.655
whenever you have a change of government,

00:53:34.655 --> 00:53:37.600
'cause most of these political
leaders at national level

00:53:40.020 --> 00:53:43.110
we don't have a merit system

00:53:43.110 --> 00:53:46.181
that exists like in the U.S.

00:53:46.181 --> 00:53:48.210
When you have a change of governments,

00:53:48.210 --> 00:53:50.040
all of the leaderships,

00:53:50.040 --> 00:53:51.750
and even a lot of the civil service,

00:53:51.750 --> 00:53:56.280
gets changed, because
they're looking for rewards

00:53:56.280 --> 00:53:59.253
for their political supporters.

00:54:00.960 --> 00:54:04.230
But, but the good side is

00:54:04.230 --> 00:54:07.443
in our traditional leadership
system, like in Sierra Leone,

00:54:09.150 --> 00:54:11.640
as much as people criticize it,

00:54:11.640 --> 00:54:14.283
our people respect chieftaincy.

00:54:15.570 --> 00:54:19.710
I'm never going to have
to stand an election

00:54:19.710 --> 00:54:21.003
for the rest of my life,

00:54:22.020 --> 00:54:25.740
because our people will trust
that their paramount chief

00:54:25.740 --> 00:54:27.630
is going to look after their interests

00:54:27.630 --> 00:54:28.463
for the rest of their life.

00:54:28.463 --> 00:54:30.240
So, on the other hand,

00:54:30.240 --> 00:54:32.640
I'm a lot more accountable to my people,

00:54:32.640 --> 00:54:35.220
and I'm just giving myself as an example,

00:54:35.220 --> 00:54:37.860
but generally our
traditional governance system

00:54:37.860 --> 00:54:39.810
is a lot more stable,

00:54:39.810 --> 00:54:42.510
otherwise, our country
will have gone to pots.

00:54:42.510 --> 00:54:45.120
So, it's not all hopeless.

00:54:45.120 --> 00:54:48.450
Even though there's a lot of
churning at the national level,

00:54:48.450 --> 00:54:53.070
there's a lot of stability
also at the local level.

00:54:53.070 --> 00:54:56.040
So it'll be good to try to leverage that.

00:54:57.471 --> 00:54:59.297
- Thank you, Paramount Chief Farley.

00:54:59.297 --> 00:55:00.130
Dr. Thiam.

00:55:03.976 --> 00:55:07.809
(Sylla Thiam speaking French)

00:56:16.603 --> 00:56:18.840
All right, thank you, Dr. Thiam.

00:56:18.840 --> 00:56:21.210
And our last question for this round

00:56:21.210 --> 00:56:25.700
before we move on to Q&amp;A.

00:56:27.060 --> 00:56:29.340
Would it be fair to say that the lack

00:56:29.340 --> 00:56:32.160
of universal primary healthcare

00:56:32.160 --> 00:56:35.373
is, in itself, a public health emergency?

00:56:36.900 --> 00:56:37.803
Dr. Dahn?

00:56:39.960 --> 00:56:41.013
- Yes.

00:56:43.081 --> 00:56:45.420
The emergency systems are normally built

00:56:45.420 --> 00:56:47.763
on the routine healthcare systems.

00:56:48.930 --> 00:56:51.660
And most of our people

00:56:51.660 --> 00:56:54.423
seek healthcare at the primary level.

00:56:56.880 --> 00:57:00.220
The primary level normally is weak,

00:57:03.600 --> 00:57:08.600
because one of the panelists earlier said

00:57:09.450 --> 00:57:13.500
our governance and the systems
are highly centralized.

00:57:13.500 --> 00:57:17.400
So you will realize that
most of the improvement

00:57:17.400 --> 00:57:20.607
that is done is done at the
central (voice cutting out).

00:57:25.980 --> 00:57:28.860
If you take hospitals for example,

00:57:28.860 --> 00:57:30.900
you'll realize that our tertiary hospital,

00:57:30.900 --> 00:57:31.890
if you come to Liberia,

00:57:31.890 --> 00:57:34.530
we have like two tertiary hospitals.

00:57:34.530 --> 00:57:37.110
The biggest one is in Monrovia.

00:57:37.110 --> 00:57:41.280
And what that brings to mind is the focus

00:57:41.280 --> 00:57:45.720
and the budget that is given
to that is by far higher

00:57:45.720 --> 00:57:48.510
than what goes to
primary healthcare level.

00:57:48.510 --> 00:57:50.670
And what that brings to mind is

00:57:50.670 --> 00:57:54.150
politicians will think about
themselves when they are ill

00:57:54.150 --> 00:57:55.383
where they want to go.

00:57:56.310 --> 00:57:59.770
And so they tend to invest in that more

00:58:00.780 --> 00:58:03.390
at the expense of the primary healthcare,

00:58:03.390 --> 00:58:04.860
where the majority of the people

00:58:04.860 --> 00:58:09.270
who voted out for them do seek healthcare.

00:58:09.270 --> 00:58:12.720
Of course, so when the
primary healthcare level

00:58:12.720 --> 00:58:16.473
is not strong enough,
when there's an epidemic,

00:58:17.550 --> 00:58:22.240
epidemic can actually (voice cutting out)

00:58:23.880 --> 00:58:25.830
and overwhelm the whole country.

00:58:25.830 --> 00:58:29.700
So the system need to
be considered as a whole

00:58:29.700 --> 00:58:34.700
and not heavily weighed
towards the elites,

00:58:35.910 --> 00:58:38.642
which is what is happening
in most of our countries.

00:58:38.642 --> 00:58:40.000
Thank you.

00:58:40.000 --> 00:58:41.430
- All right, thank you, Dr. Dahn.

00:58:41.430 --> 00:58:45.420
It is 1:02, and I want to
get to the Q&amp;A section,

00:58:45.420 --> 00:58:48.905
'cause we have so much
engagement from the audience.

00:58:48.905 --> 00:58:52.530
So Paramount Chief Farley and Dr. Thiam,

00:58:52.530 --> 00:58:54.630
could you please keep your response brief?

00:58:57.000 --> 00:58:59.100
- Oh yeah.
- Paramount Chief Farley.

00:58:59.100 --> 00:59:01.560
- Primary health, we don't have

00:59:01.560 --> 00:59:05.013
much of a primary
healthcare to start with,

00:59:05.873 --> 00:59:07.230
because the components

00:59:07.230 --> 00:59:10.200
that go into delivering
primary healthcare.

00:59:10.200 --> 00:59:14.520
We have one of those
components that we have nurses,

00:59:14.520 --> 00:59:19.520
and, you know, auxiliary health workers

00:59:19.860 --> 00:59:23.403
and even community health officials.

00:59:25.740 --> 00:59:29.520
Most people know how to
diagnose these diseases,

00:59:29.520 --> 00:59:31.560
but that's not the real problem.

00:59:31.560 --> 00:59:32.523
When you go there,

00:59:34.410 --> 00:59:37.425
they are going to inevitably ask for money

00:59:37.425 --> 00:59:41.490
that our people don't
have to buy medicines.

00:59:41.490 --> 00:59:45.003
And some of them actually
ask for consultations.

00:59:46.538 --> 00:59:51.538
And so most people just
don't get primary healthcare,

00:59:51.900 --> 00:59:54.180
even though on paper you have

00:59:54.180 --> 00:59:58.383
all of these community health
offices, health centers built,

00:59:59.220 --> 01:00:01.560
but if you don't have money,

01:00:01.560 --> 01:00:04.710
which most of our rural people don't have,

01:00:04.710 --> 01:00:06.603
then they don't go there.

01:00:07.470 --> 01:00:10.170
And so you have these
auxiliary units coming in,

01:00:10.170 --> 01:00:12.210
and it's very dysfunctional.

01:00:12.210 --> 01:00:14.820
So we don't have much
of a primary healthcare,

01:00:14.820 --> 01:00:19.820
and yes, that is endemic,
that is an epidemic,

01:00:20.010 --> 01:00:21.780
because, as I said-
Thank you.

01:00:21.780 --> 01:00:25.530
- Yeah, so that's a point I want to make.

01:00:26.520 --> 01:00:28.514
- Thank you, Paramount Chief Farley.

01:00:28.514 --> 01:00:29.681
And Dr. Thiam?

01:00:33.570 --> 01:00:37.403
(Sylla Thiam speaking French)

01:01:58.440 --> 01:02:00.360
Thank you, Dr. Thiam.

01:02:00.360 --> 01:02:02.250
So now I'm looking through the chat

01:02:02.250 --> 01:02:04.140
and seeing the questions

01:02:04.140 --> 01:02:06.480
that were sent to me from the audience.

01:02:06.480 --> 01:02:10.117
I see this first one here
is directed to Dr. Dahn.

01:02:10.117 --> 01:02:11.737
"From your experience

01:02:11.737 --> 01:02:14.857
"as the former Minister
of Health in Liberia,

01:02:14.857 --> 01:02:17.917
"how can international
funding be structured

01:02:17.917 --> 01:02:21.277
"to not only address
immediate health crises,

01:02:21.277 --> 01:02:23.407
"but also contribute to the establishment

01:02:23.407 --> 01:02:25.537
"of enduring healthcare systems?

01:02:25.537 --> 01:02:28.297
"Are there successful models or examples

01:02:28.297 --> 01:02:32.107
"where INGOs have played a pivotal role

01:02:32.107 --> 01:02:34.567
"building long-term
healthcare infrastructure

01:02:34.567 --> 01:02:37.557
"in collaboration with local entities?"

01:02:40.140 --> 01:02:41.583
- Okay, thank you, yes.

01:02:44.613 --> 01:02:48.900
As a country, the first thing
is to provide the directions

01:02:48.900 --> 01:02:53.043
for the country and for
those who want to help you.

01:02:55.920 --> 01:03:00.450
So countries normally will
develop strategic plans

01:03:00.450 --> 01:03:05.450
and policies to provide
directions for the country.

01:03:07.320 --> 01:03:11.730
Take for instance, the
health sector policy strategy

01:03:11.730 --> 01:03:13.950
on where you want to
take the health sector

01:03:13.950 --> 01:03:16.683
over a period of time,
maybe five to 10 years.

01:03:19.140 --> 01:03:23.261
Now, implementing the policy

01:03:23.261 --> 01:03:25.290
and the strategy is another thing,

01:03:25.290 --> 01:03:28.350
because many times people
develop policy strategies

01:03:28.350 --> 01:03:32.613
and it's a document and it is
sitting and accumulating dust.

01:03:33.810 --> 01:03:37.173
So, working with,

01:03:40.410 --> 01:03:42.180
especially the decentralized level

01:03:42.180 --> 01:03:44.403
where the implementation is actually done,

01:03:45.360 --> 01:03:48.183
normally we do an operational
plan, which is annual.

01:03:49.620 --> 01:03:52.440
And in doing that operational plan

01:03:52.440 --> 01:03:53.800
is to sit with

01:03:55.920 --> 01:03:58.920
all your implementing
partners within the county

01:03:58.920 --> 01:04:01.920
and plan to align their resources

01:04:01.920 --> 01:04:06.033
to the plan to be able to implement.

01:04:07.316 --> 01:04:08.700
Now with my experience,

01:04:08.700 --> 01:04:11.070
the first thing is if they plan

01:04:11.070 --> 01:04:15.030
the national plans and
policies are done together,

01:04:15.030 --> 01:04:18.300
it provides the opportunity
for donor partners

01:04:18.300 --> 01:04:23.300
to also be able to align their
resources to your priority.

01:04:23.700 --> 01:04:25.023
So that's the first thing.

01:04:25.860 --> 01:04:27.993
Now those resources,

01:04:29.130 --> 01:04:32.220
when it comes to the implementation level,

01:04:32.220 --> 01:04:35.590
those resources also
need to align properly

01:04:36.570 --> 01:04:41.340
to the operational plans
that you have in country,

01:04:41.340 --> 01:04:44.220
and jointly you can monitor together.

01:04:44.220 --> 01:04:46.980
So in Liberia, what we did was to

01:04:46.980 --> 01:04:51.980
organize the Health Sector
Coordination Committee, the HSCC,

01:04:52.320 --> 01:04:57.320
which is where the health sector
policy decisions are made.

01:04:58.800 --> 01:05:02.520
It is normally chaired by
the Minister of Health,

01:05:02.520 --> 01:05:04.097
co-chaired by the (voice cutting out).

01:05:05.653 --> 01:05:09.180
All the donor partners are present there,

01:05:09.180 --> 01:05:12.630
and they tend to give
inputs into the strategy

01:05:12.630 --> 01:05:13.463
and the policy,

01:05:15.310 --> 01:05:17.310
assign some of their technicians

01:05:17.310 --> 01:05:19.380
during the development process.

01:05:19.380 --> 01:05:22.874
And so when they are
planning their support,

01:05:22.874 --> 01:05:27.063
they plan along those lines
for us and that works.

01:05:27.900 --> 01:05:30.000
Now, there are other examples.

01:05:30.000 --> 01:05:33.840
I did mention earlier weak
systems will continue to be weak.

01:05:33.840 --> 01:05:38.310
We've had situations where
donor partners work with us

01:05:38.310 --> 01:05:40.560
to build financial management systems.

01:05:40.560 --> 01:05:42.690
For example, currently I'm

01:05:42.690 --> 01:05:44.670
at the University of Liberia

01:05:44.670 --> 01:05:47.070
at the College of Health Sciences.

01:05:47.070 --> 01:05:49.803
Before I got there in 2019,

01:05:50.700 --> 01:05:52.960
the College of Health Sciences

01:05:53.850 --> 01:05:57.570
had never managed $500 budget.

01:05:57.570 --> 01:06:00.240
Everything was highly centralized.

01:06:00.240 --> 01:06:02.560
But this is an institution where

01:06:05.460 --> 01:06:08.910
faculty members need to
write research grants.

01:06:08.910 --> 01:06:09.960
They need to do research,

01:06:09.960 --> 01:06:12.300
they need to publish for
their own professional group.

01:06:12.300 --> 01:06:13.923
The systems were not there.

01:06:14.820 --> 01:06:16.590
We were supported by,

01:06:16.590 --> 01:06:21.590
with donor funding by
another U.S. institutions

01:06:22.740 --> 01:06:25.950
to build the Office of Sponsored Research,

01:06:25.950 --> 01:06:29.310
to build the Office of
Financial Management.

01:06:29.310 --> 01:06:31.800
We have a monitoring and evaluation system

01:06:31.800 --> 01:06:34.260
in place at the college,

01:06:34.260 --> 01:06:37.530
and we have a Human Resource Office,

01:06:37.530 --> 01:06:39.390
we have a Communication Office.

01:06:39.390 --> 01:06:41.310
Those systems were not there.

01:06:41.310 --> 01:06:44.310
And so when people want to support you,

01:06:44.310 --> 01:06:46.380
they don't see those systems there.

01:06:46.380 --> 01:06:49.290
It becomes risky to put in their money.

01:06:49.290 --> 01:06:53.340
Since we started in 2019 to present,

01:06:53.340 --> 01:06:56.490
we've managed over $10 million,

01:06:56.490 --> 01:06:59.730
or we are managing over
$10 million at the moment,

01:06:59.730 --> 01:07:01.860
is because the systems are there.

01:07:01.860 --> 01:07:04.350
Of course, with the system currently,

01:07:04.350 --> 01:07:07.620
we are not independent yet.

01:07:07.620 --> 01:07:09.690
We are still working with the partners

01:07:09.690 --> 01:07:13.980
and growing the system
until we can take on fully.

01:07:13.980 --> 01:07:17.640
But most of the work is
done within our system,

01:07:17.640 --> 01:07:20.670
and they have high level
supervision for it.

01:07:20.670 --> 01:07:22.860
And I think that is a very good model,

01:07:22.860 --> 01:07:24.510
because at the end of the day

01:07:24.510 --> 01:07:26.220
when the international
partner is not there,

01:07:26.220 --> 01:07:29.190
that system continues
within the university.

01:07:29.190 --> 01:07:30.540
Thank you.

01:07:30.540 --> 01:07:32.467
- Thank you, Dr. Dahn.

01:07:32.467 --> 01:07:37.117
"Do you think that NGOs cause
mismanagement of resources

01:07:37.117 --> 01:07:40.027
"as a whole or individuals with them?

01:07:40.027 --> 01:07:42.097
"How can we ensure that resources

01:07:42.097 --> 01:07:44.737
"go directly to the community leaders

01:07:44.737 --> 01:07:48.330
"to aid local support and governance?"

01:07:48.330 --> 01:07:50.913
Dr. Thiam, could you answer this one?

01:07:53.292 --> 01:07:58.292
- Okay, I think (speaking in French).

01:09:46.710 --> 01:09:47.760
- Thank you, Dr. Thiam.

01:09:47.760 --> 01:09:51.397
And I see a question here
for Paramount Chief Farley.

01:09:51.397 --> 01:09:55.657
"How can donor agencies and
international organizations

01:09:55.657 --> 01:09:57.937
"deal directly with local leaders

01:09:57.937 --> 01:10:00.427
"in managing situations like these?

01:10:00.427 --> 01:10:03.657
"Do government policies allow for this?"

01:10:06.000 --> 01:10:07.680
- Not directly.

01:10:07.680 --> 01:10:08.763
If it doesn't,

01:10:12.300 --> 01:10:14.130
there's a huge dysfunction

01:10:14.130 --> 01:10:16.960
in a lot of our post-colonial countries

01:10:18.240 --> 01:10:20.980
where things are very centralized

01:10:22.140 --> 01:10:23.613
in the seat of government.

01:10:26.606 --> 01:10:29.673
One work around that you can develop,

01:10:30.630 --> 01:10:32.280
I'd already mentioned this before,

01:10:32.280 --> 01:10:33.573
but I'll say it again.

01:10:35.550 --> 01:10:38.460
Yes, it's a good thing to,

01:10:38.460 --> 01:10:41.970
if you have credible NGOs,

01:10:41.970 --> 01:10:43.620
I mean really credible NGOs,

01:10:43.620 --> 01:10:47.580
that are really accountable
and who they can deal,

01:10:47.580 --> 01:10:48.960
yes, the central government

01:10:48.960 --> 01:10:51.870
will give them general authority
to operate in the country,

01:10:51.870 --> 01:10:56.870
but they can deal directly
with the traditional leaders

01:10:57.144 --> 01:11:02.144
who are best trusted by
the communities to deliver.

01:11:03.570 --> 01:11:07.260
And so the model that I would suggest

01:11:07.260 --> 01:11:12.040
is large international organizations

01:11:14.160 --> 01:11:17.940
as to work with their local partners

01:11:17.940 --> 01:11:21.183
to deal directly with the
traditional authorities.

01:11:22.320 --> 01:11:24.060
The government is already getting,

01:11:24.060 --> 01:11:26.040
although they always say it's not enough,

01:11:26.040 --> 01:11:28.050
the government is already
getting a lot of money

01:11:28.050 --> 01:11:30.690
from place like the World Bank,

01:11:30.690 --> 01:11:32.370
the African Development Bank.

01:11:32.370 --> 01:11:37.370
They use a sovereign capacity

01:11:37.590 --> 01:11:42.590
to get funding for a lot of things.

01:11:42.660 --> 01:11:45.090
But a lot of that funding

01:11:45.090 --> 01:11:48.330
doesn't filter down to the communities.

01:11:48.330 --> 01:11:50.190
The best way to get it to filter down

01:11:50.190 --> 01:11:52.620
is to get a credible NGO

01:11:52.620 --> 01:11:55.650
and require them to deal directly

01:11:55.650 --> 01:11:59.050
with the traditional leaders

01:12:00.389 --> 01:12:04.770
who will guide them a lot better.

01:12:04.770 --> 01:12:08.310
And trust me, the communities
will benefit a lot more,

01:12:08.310 --> 01:12:09.897
because those traditional leaders

01:12:09.897 --> 01:12:13.030
are a lot more accountable
to their communities

01:12:13.030 --> 01:12:15.270
than are national governments.

01:12:15.270 --> 01:12:17.610
Of course, the national
governments still play a role

01:12:17.610 --> 01:12:21.480
in the world coordination
of registration of NGOs.

01:12:21.480 --> 01:12:23.433
But on a project-by-project basis,

01:12:25.260 --> 01:12:28.680
the most efficient way
of impacting communities

01:12:28.680 --> 01:12:30.447
is through their traditional leaders

01:12:30.447 --> 01:12:33.760
and through NGOs and through
their traditional leaders.

01:12:34.810 --> 01:12:37.050
- Okay, thank you, Paramount Chief Farley.

01:12:37.050 --> 01:12:39.900
I see Dr. Dahn has two fingers up.

01:12:39.900 --> 01:12:41.500
So the floor is yours, Dr. Dahn?

01:12:42.557 --> 01:12:46.170
- Yes, I just want to
complement what he said.

01:12:46.170 --> 01:12:51.170
It is possible to support
local governments directly.

01:12:54.240 --> 01:12:58.860
The same way I complained
about donor partners

01:13:00.420 --> 01:13:03.150
falling on not being very transparent

01:13:03.150 --> 01:13:05.820
is the same thing that
happens with the government,

01:13:05.820 --> 01:13:10.820
and the central governments calling on

01:13:10.920 --> 01:13:14.163
and not letting go to
the local governments.

01:13:15.030 --> 01:13:17.820
Ideally, if you want that to happen,

01:13:17.820 --> 01:13:19.920
the local government system,

01:13:19.920 --> 01:13:21.960
the capacity need to be built.

01:13:21.960 --> 01:13:25.680
The same systems you
have at the central level

01:13:25.680 --> 01:13:29.340
needs to be in the local government office

01:13:29.340 --> 01:13:34.203
and the different agencies
well built within their local.

01:13:35.610 --> 01:13:38.970
Take for instance the
counties within the county,

01:13:38.970 --> 01:13:43.970
which most of you will call
districts, and then they report,

01:13:44.700 --> 01:13:46.920
so the local government office

01:13:46.920 --> 01:13:50.310
who is headed by the superintendent.

01:13:50.310 --> 01:13:53.700
When you look at Liberia,
you'll realize that some of the

01:13:53.700 --> 01:13:57.183
services are decentralized to some extent.

01:13:58.020 --> 01:13:59.310
But one institution

01:13:59.310 --> 01:14:02.730
that will not decentralize
is Finance Ministry.

01:14:02.730 --> 01:14:07.730
They tend to all own and
don't want to let go.

01:14:08.010 --> 01:14:10.950
And it's difficult to understand why,

01:14:10.950 --> 01:14:15.610
because the people who work
at the local government level

01:14:17.010 --> 01:14:19.173
have went to the same schools you went to.

01:14:20.290 --> 01:14:22.080
(voice muffling) People
graduated from the same schools

01:14:22.080 --> 01:14:23.940
and you have the same degrees.

01:14:23.940 --> 01:14:25.020
- Yes.
- It happens

01:14:25.020 --> 01:14:27.360
in the health sector the same way.

01:14:27.360 --> 01:14:30.000
In the health sector
you'll see one program

01:14:30.000 --> 01:14:31.617
in immunization, for example.

01:14:31.617 --> 01:14:35.070
The will forever be training of trainers.

01:14:35.070 --> 01:14:37.380
They go to the local
level, they train trainers.

01:14:37.380 --> 01:14:41.340
You expect that the people
they train are trainers

01:14:41.340 --> 01:14:42.720
will scale down the training,

01:14:42.720 --> 01:14:47.587
but the people at the central
will continue to go down

01:14:47.587 --> 01:14:52.260
to the county and the district
to continue to do training.

01:14:52.260 --> 01:14:54.090
So as a result, the trainers they train

01:14:54.090 --> 01:14:56.460
can never have the opportunity to train,

01:14:56.460 --> 01:14:57.810
but it takes the government

01:14:57.810 --> 01:15:02.310
to make a serious
decision to decentralize.

01:15:02.310 --> 01:15:05.190
It's easier to decentralize
with all the other ministries,

01:15:05.190 --> 01:15:09.270
but they need to look
at the Finance Ministry

01:15:09.270 --> 01:15:11.880
and see how they can
decentralize Finance Ministry

01:15:11.880 --> 01:15:13.560
to support the local government,

01:15:13.560 --> 01:15:15.810
and all the other things will follow.

01:15:15.810 --> 01:15:18.243
That is very difficult
to do in our setting.

01:15:20.411 --> 01:15:22.254
- All right, Paramount Chief Farley,

01:15:22.254 --> 01:15:26.079
you had two fingers?
- Yeah, just to buttress.

01:15:26.079 --> 01:15:31.079
I am a professional engineer
for over four decades.

01:15:31.080 --> 01:15:34.200
So my bent is more to
see the world as it is

01:15:34.200 --> 01:15:35.283
rather than to,

01:15:36.493 --> 01:15:40.710
and say how will I get things done

01:15:40.710 --> 01:15:42.513
based on how the world is.

01:15:43.350 --> 01:15:47.100
As Dr. Dahn said, no Ministry of Finance

01:15:47.100 --> 01:15:50.730
is going to decentralize.

01:15:50.730 --> 01:15:53.433
No matter what the World
Bank or anybody else says.

01:15:54.750 --> 01:15:56.550
I can give lots of examples.

01:15:56.550 --> 01:15:58.500
I'm not even going to bother with that.

01:16:00.032 --> 01:16:04.623
So the work around that
is work with these...

01:16:06.937 --> 01:16:10.020
The donor partners can fund NGOs directly,

01:16:10.020 --> 01:16:13.380
but let them be reputable NGOs

01:16:13.380 --> 01:16:18.380
and let the donor partners get
feedback, monitoring systems,

01:16:20.100 --> 01:16:22.320
that ensures that they have, in fact,

01:16:22.320 --> 01:16:24.330
dealt with the traditional leaders

01:16:24.330 --> 01:16:26.580
who are trusted by their communities.

01:16:26.580 --> 01:16:29.250
I think that there's no way
government can stop that

01:16:29.250 --> 01:16:32.640
as long as the the NGOs
are working in the country.

01:16:32.640 --> 01:16:34.920
But to say that you're hoping,

01:16:34.920 --> 01:16:38.880
because the World Bank has been
after our government forever

01:16:38.880 --> 01:16:41.760
to decentralized, and they even have

01:16:41.760 --> 01:16:44.190
a permanent decentralization secretariat

01:16:44.190 --> 01:16:45.660
and nothing yet decentralized.

01:16:45.660 --> 01:16:47.910
So instead of following that,

01:16:47.910 --> 01:16:50.580
just go ahead and walk around it,

01:16:50.580 --> 01:16:52.470
walk through these international NGOs,

01:16:52.470 --> 01:16:54.390
get them to be credible

01:16:54.390 --> 01:16:56.070
and walk directly with those leaders.

01:16:56.070 --> 01:16:57.303
Nobody can stop that.

01:16:58.470 --> 01:17:01.080
- Thank you, Paramount Chief Farley.

01:17:01.080 --> 01:17:03.487
A next question from the audience says,

01:17:03.487 --> 01:17:07.417
"Looking into future
outbreaks, what can technology,

01:17:07.417 --> 01:17:10.027
"digital health, telemedicine,

01:17:10.027 --> 01:17:11.977
"or connectivity be used

01:17:11.977 --> 01:17:15.540
"to improve the handling of outbreaks?"

01:17:15.540 --> 01:17:18.483
So, Dr. Thiam, I think
we can start with you.

01:17:20.698 --> 01:17:24.615
(Sylla Thiam speaking French)

01:19:09.927 --> 01:19:12.817
One question, so we have
only a five-minute warning.

01:19:12.817 --> 01:19:15.457
"How should external actors,

01:19:15.457 --> 01:19:19.357
"whether they be bilateral
or international NGOs,

01:19:19.357 --> 01:19:23.017
"navigate the degrees
of tension or separation

01:19:23.017 --> 01:19:26.167
"between traditional government
and central government?

01:19:26.167 --> 01:19:29.317
"Typically there is for outsiders

01:19:29.317 --> 01:19:32.197
"a need to go through central government

01:19:32.197 --> 01:19:35.857
"in order to get to the
traditional government,

01:19:35.857 --> 01:19:39.937
"which has both financial and
programmatic implications.

01:19:39.937 --> 01:19:43.117
"So for those of us who
desire to work more closely

01:19:43.117 --> 01:19:46.237
"with local communities
and traditional leaders,

01:19:46.237 --> 01:19:50.130
"what advice would you
give for navigating this?"

01:19:50.130 --> 01:19:52.533
So Paramount Chief Farley,
we'll start with you.

01:19:54.030 --> 01:19:55.110
- You obviously need to register.

01:19:55.110 --> 01:19:58.200
Once you're registered
as an NGO in the country,

01:19:58.200 --> 01:20:00.630
your program itself, you can develop

01:20:00.630 --> 01:20:04.743
over how you develop and
implement your programs,

01:20:05.730 --> 01:20:07.440
but obviously you have to

01:20:07.440 --> 01:20:10.170
register with some central agencies.

01:20:10.170 --> 01:20:12.363
That's why it's a good workaround.

01:20:13.380 --> 01:20:16.440
You're not required to
implement all of your programs

01:20:16.440 --> 01:20:17.580
through the central government.

01:20:17.580 --> 01:20:22.050
In fact, most NGOs
develop their own programs

01:20:22.050 --> 01:20:24.270
and deal directly with local governments.

01:20:24.270 --> 01:20:27.840
The problem is getting
the dependable NGOs,

01:20:27.840 --> 01:20:31.830
because there's also a lot
of malfeasance within NGOs.

01:20:31.830 --> 01:20:36.150
A lot of them spend a good
percent of their funds

01:20:36.150 --> 01:20:39.960
just having meetings and
meetings and meetings on end,

01:20:39.960 --> 01:20:44.220
which their own staff usually attends,

01:20:44.220 --> 01:20:45.507
get a lot of per diems,

01:20:45.507 --> 01:20:48.840
and most of that is
spent on that overhead,

01:20:48.840 --> 01:20:51.960
rather than on actually
programmatic things

01:20:51.960 --> 01:20:53.430
within the communities.

01:20:53.430 --> 01:20:56.820
But I'm sure the best NGOs will find a way

01:20:56.820 --> 01:21:01.820
of delivering most of the
funding to the program itself.

01:21:03.330 --> 01:21:04.560
But I can assure you,

01:21:04.560 --> 01:21:07.113
just to build on something,
Dr. Thiam had said,

01:21:09.000 --> 01:21:10.140
right at independence,

01:21:10.140 --> 01:21:11.340
there was a huge imbalance

01:21:11.340 --> 01:21:13.560
between the level of
education and sophistication

01:21:13.560 --> 01:21:15.690
between those in central government

01:21:15.690 --> 01:21:18.900
and those in traditional institutions.

01:21:18.900 --> 01:21:19.953
Not anymore.

01:21:21.390 --> 01:21:24.240
Among paramount chiefs,
you have medical doctors,

01:21:24.240 --> 01:21:25.770
you have lawyers.

01:21:25.770 --> 01:21:27.150
I'm a professional engineer.

01:21:27.150 --> 01:21:29.880
I'm a registered professional engineer.

01:21:29.880 --> 01:21:33.600
I've also had done my masters
in public administration

01:21:33.600 --> 01:21:35.040
and business administration,

01:21:35.040 --> 01:21:38.100
and a lot of times we you actually have

01:21:38.100 --> 01:21:41.250
a lot more capable resources

01:21:41.250 --> 01:21:43.890
at the traditional governance level

01:21:43.890 --> 01:21:46.890
than at the central government,
the government level.

01:21:46.890 --> 01:21:51.390
So, working through those
NGOs, get the NGOs registered,

01:21:51.390 --> 01:21:53.730
but don't hesitate to work directly

01:21:53.730 --> 01:21:56.970
with the traditional leaders, because,

01:21:56.970 --> 01:22:00.253
you know, we are getting
more and more sophisticated.

01:22:00.253 --> 01:22:03.120
- All right, thank you,
Paramount Chief Farley.

01:22:03.120 --> 01:22:06.600
And our last question will go to Dr. Dahn,

01:22:06.600 --> 01:22:09.097
as we only have one minute left.

01:22:09.097 --> 01:22:12.067
"What would you have done
differently to manage the crisis

01:22:12.067 --> 01:22:14.917
"in terms of managing
resource mobilization,

01:22:14.917 --> 01:22:19.267
"distribution channels to get
it to who needs it the most?

01:22:19.267 --> 01:22:21.247
"And the problems of trust and credibility

01:22:21.247 --> 01:22:24.600
"between citizens, NGOs,
and government players?"

01:22:24.600 --> 01:22:27.480
So the last minute is yours, Dr. Dahn.

01:22:27.480 --> 01:22:31.863
- Okay, for me, I will work in
building governance capacity.

01:22:33.120 --> 01:22:35.400
I will work with them
in building the systems

01:22:35.400 --> 01:22:37.560
that's supposed to be built for them

01:22:37.560 --> 01:22:39.820
to be able to manage their own resources

01:22:40.778 --> 01:22:42.243
plus donor resources.

01:22:43.080 --> 01:22:47.940
An example is with the USAID funding

01:22:47.940 --> 01:22:50.190
that is being managed by
the Ministry of Health,

01:22:50.190 --> 01:22:52.443
the fixed amount reimbursement agreements.

01:22:53.940 --> 01:22:56.280
This is an agreement between USAID

01:22:56.280 --> 01:22:58.443
and the Ministry of Health.

01:23:01.210 --> 01:23:04.293
(voice cutting out)

01:23:14.897 --> 01:23:18.992
- Okay, well unfortunately
because of the connectivity,

01:23:18.992 --> 01:23:23.670
and it is our final minute
here, we do have to end.

01:23:23.670 --> 01:23:26.730
So I just want to say, you know,

01:23:26.730 --> 01:23:30.030
thank you to our panelists
for a fantastic discussion,

01:23:30.030 --> 01:23:32.430
and thank you all for joining us today.

01:23:32.430 --> 01:23:34.230
We also wanna thank our sponsors,

01:23:34.230 --> 01:23:36.870
the Yale Institute for Global
Health, the Walker Fund,

01:23:36.870 --> 01:23:38.730
and the Council on African Studies

01:23:38.730 --> 01:23:41.550
at the MacMillan Center at Yale.

01:23:41.550 --> 01:23:43.980
The session was recorded
and will be posted

01:23:43.980 --> 01:23:46.020
on the Institutes for Global Health's

01:23:46.020 --> 01:23:49.080
Conversation Series playlist on YouTube.

01:23:49.080 --> 01:23:51.240
We look forward to seeing
you at the next event

01:23:51.240 --> 01:23:53.910
of the Global Health Conversation series.

01:23:53.910 --> 01:23:55.310
Thank you all and take care.

01:23:56.520 --> 01:23:57.976
- Thank you.

01:23:57.976 --> 01:24:00.073
- And thank you for
providing the opportunity.

01:24:00.073 --> 01:24:02.070
I hope it was helpful.

01:24:02.070 --> 01:24:03.220
- It's been a pleasure.

